  "query_preview": "..(Sunday through Friday)\nIssued at 339 PM EST FRI NOV 20 2026\n\nThe long-term model suite analysis shows a more eventful weather\npattern for the last full week of November. Upper-level ridging of\nlate will now be mostly off the Atlantic Seaboard at the start of\nthe period, leaving broader southwest flow aloft across the Ohio \nValley ahead of a 500 hPa trough passing over the Plains. Eastern\nKentucky will initially be situated between a surface low \n(perhaps subtropical or tropical in nature) lifting along the \nSoutheast Coast, bringing rainfall to the Carolinas and adjacent \nareas, and a cold front stretched from the Upper Great Lakes to \nTexas. Further upstream, a robust ~530 dam is spinning off the \nPacific Northwest Coast.\n\nSubstantial mid- and high-level cloud cover will likely be over \neastern Kentucky during the first half of the day on Sunday as the\nlow moves up the coast. Some of the models do brush far eastern \nPike County with a little QPF Sunday morning, though confidence in\nthis occurring is low - the HRRR/RAP show precip staying well \neast of the area while REFS/GFS/ECMWF do support the idea of some\nlight QPF. Either way, any impact should be minimal to nil as \ndeeper moisture lifts away Sunday afternoon ahead of the cold \nfront approaching from the west. Models are then in good agreement\nshowing that cold front pushing through Sunday night into early \nMonday, though with little meaningful rainfall as the corridor of \nelevated PWATs will be narrow and the forcing upper-level trough \nwill be dampening. Probabilities for greater than 0.25 inch are \nlow (0 to 20 percent). Aside from the rainfall, the most notable \nchange with the cold front will be the turn toward notably cooler \ntemperatures. Ahead of the front, mean LREF 850 hPa temperatures \nwill be around 10C on Sunday, but then dip to 1 to 5C by Tuesday \nmorning as shortwave ridging returns aloft and high pressure passes\nover the Great Lakes.\n\nThe forecast becomes substantially less certain from midweek onward\nas remnants of the Pacific upper low transit the Rockies as a\ntrough before attempting to dig and redevelop into a closed low\nnear or over the Mississippi Valley by late Wednesday into early\nThursday. Solutions vary widely with respect to the strength as\nwell as track of the upper-level low, leaving the forecast for\nWednesday onward uncertain. This has a significant bearing on the\nsensible weather for Thanksgiving Day and Friday with solutions \nranging from cold and wet to cool and mostly dry other than brief \nrain chances with another cold front.\n\nIn sensible terms, look for one more mild day on Sunday with\nincreasing sunshine (after any light rain in the morning over the\neast) and very mild highs in the mid 60s to around 70F. Clouds \nincrease again late Sunday and Sunday night with the subsequent \narrival of mainly light rain shower chances overnight. It will be \nbreezy and milder than recent nights with lows mainly in the 40s\nto around 50F. Looking ahead to Monday, rain showers will taper \noff from west to east as winds turn to the northwest and take on a\ncharacteristic chill for late November. Highs will only be in the\n50s. Dry conditions with near-normal high temperatures in the 50s\nand lows mainly in the 30s follow from Monday night through late \nTuesday night. Near- to slightly below-normal temperatures remain \nlikely for Wednesday through Friday, but specifics on when and how\nmuch rainfall might occur remain hazy.",
